 (16502,2,'Arise and deliver thy warning!',NULL,74,2),
 (16503,3,'Arise and warn!',NULL,74,2),
 (16504,1,'And your Lord do magnify,',NULL,74,3),
 (16505,2,'And thy Lord do thou magnify!',NULL,74,3),
 (16506,3,'Thy Lord magnify,',NULL,74,3),
 (16507,1,'And your garments do purify,',NULL,74,4),
 (16508,2,'And thy garments keep free from stain!',NULL,74,4),
 (16509,3,'Thy raiment purify,',NULL,74,4),
 (16510,1,'And uncleanness do shun,',NULL,74,5),
 (16511,2,'And all abomination shun!',NULL,74,5),
 (16512,3,'Pollution shun!',NULL,74,5),
 (16513,1,'And bestow not favors that you may receive again with increase,',NULL,74,6),
 (16514,2,'Nor expect, in giving, any increase (for thyself)!',NULL,74,6),
 (16515,3,'And show not favour, seeking wordly gain!',NULL,74,6),
 (16516,1,'And for the sake of your Lord, be patient.',NULL,74,7),
 (16517,2,'But, for thy Lord''s (Cause), be patient and constant!',NULL,74,7),
 (16518,3,'For the sake of thy Lord, be patient!',NULL,74,7),
 (16519,1,'For when the trumpet is sounded,',NULL,74,8),
 (16520,2,'Finally, when the Trumpet is sounded,',NULL,74,8),
 (16521,3,'For when the trumpet shall sound,',NULL,74,8),
 (16522,1,'That, at that time, shall be a difficult day,',NULL,74,9),
 (16523,2,'That will be- that Day - a Day of Distress,-',NULL,74,9),
 (16524,3,'Surely that day will be a day of anguish,',NULL,74,9),
 (16525,1,'For the unbelievers, anything but easy.',NULL,74,10),
 (16526,2,'Far from easy for those without Faith.',NULL,74,10),
 (16527,3,'Not of ease, for disbelievers.',NULL,74,10),
 (16528,1,'Leave Me and him whom I created alone,',NULL,74,11),
 (16529,2,'Leave Me alone, (to deal) with the (creature) whom I created (bare and) alone!-',NULL,74,11),
 (16530,3,'Leave Me (to deal) with him whom I created lonely,',NULL,74,11),
 (16531,1,'And give him vast riches,',NULL,74,12),
 (16532,2,'To whom I granted resources in abundance,',NULL,74,12),
 (16533,3,'And then bestowed upon him ample means,',NULL,74,12),
 (16534,1,'And sons dwelling in his presence,',NULL,74,13),
 (16535,2,'And sons to be by his side!-',NULL,74,13),
 (16536,3,'And sons abiding in his presence',NULL,74,13),
 (16537,1,'And I adjusted affairs for him adjustably;',NULL,74,14),
 (16538,2,'To whom I made (life) smooth and comfortable!',NULL,74,14),
 (16539,3,'And made (life) smooth for him.',NULL,74,14),
 (16540,1,'And yet he desires that I should add more!',NULL,74,15),
 (16541,2,'Yet is he greedy-that I should add (yet more);-',NULL,74,15),
 (16542,3,'Yet he desireth that I should give more.',NULL,74,15),
 (16543,1,'By no means! surely he offers opposition to Our communications.',NULL,74,16),
 (16544,2,'By no means! For to Our Signs he has been refractory!',NULL,74,16),
 (16545,3,'Nay! For lo! he hath been stubborn to Our revelations.',NULL,74,16),
 (16546,1,'I will make a distressing punishment overtake him.',NULL,74,17),
 (16547,2,'Soon will I visit him with a mount of calamities!',NULL,74,17),
 (16548,3,'On him I shall impose a fearful doom.',NULL,74,17),
 (16549,1,'Surely he reflected and guessed,',NULL,74,18),
 (16550,2,'For he thought and he plotted;-',NULL,74,18),
 (16551,3,'For lo! he did consider; then he planned -',NULL,74,18),
 (16552,1,'But may he be cursed how he plotted;',NULL,74,19),
 (16553,2,'And woe to him! How he plotted!-',NULL,74,19),
 (16554,3,'(Self-)destroyed is he, how he planned!',NULL,74,19),
 (16555,1,'Again, may he be cursed how he plotted;',NULL,74,20),
 (16556,2,'Yea, Woe to him; How he plotted!-',NULL,74,20),
 (16557,3,'Again (self-)destroyed is he, how he planned! -',NULL,74,20),
 (16558,1,'Then he looked,',NULL,74,21),
 (16559,2,'Then he looked round;',NULL,74,21),
 (16560,3,'Then looked he,',NULL,74,21),
 (16561,1,'Then he frowned and scowled,',NULL,74,22),
 (16562,2,'Then he frowned and he scowled;',NULL,74,22),
 (16563,3,'Then frowned he and showed displeasure.',NULL,74,22),
 (16564,1,'Then he turned back and was big with pride,',NULL,74,23),
 (16565,2,'Then he turned back and was haughty;',NULL,74,23),
 (16566,3,'Then turned he away in pride',NULL,74,23),
 (16567,1,'~Then he said: This is naught but enchantment, narrated (from others);',NULL,74,24),
 (16568,2,'Then said he: "This is nothing but magic, derived from of old;',NULL,74,24),
 (16569,3,'And said: This is naught else than magic from of old;',NULL,74,24),
 (16570,1,'This is naught but the word of a mortal.',NULL,74,25),
 (16571,2,'"This is nothing but the word of a mortal!"',NULL,74,25),
 (16572,3,'This is naught else than speech of mortal man.',NULL,74,25),
 (16573,1,'I will cast him into hell.',NULL,74,26),
 (16574,2,'Soon will I cast him into Hell-Fire!',NULL,74,26),
 (16575,3,'Him shall I fling unto the burning.',NULL,74,26),
 (16576,1,'And what will make you realize what hell is?',NULL,74,27),
 (16577,2,'And what will explain to thee what Hell-Fire is?',NULL,74,27),
 (16578,3,'- Ah, what will convey unto thee what that burning is! -',NULL,74,27),
 (16579,1,'It leaves naught nor does it spare aught.',NULL,74,28),
 (16580,2,'Naught doth it permit to endure, and naught doth it leave alone!-',NULL,74,28),
 (16581,3,'It leaveth naught; it spareth naught',NULL,74,28),
 (16582,1,'It scorches the mortal.',NULL,74,29),
 (16583,2,'Darkening and changing the colour of man!',NULL,74,29),
 (16584,3,'It shrivelleth the man.',NULL,74,29),
 (16585,1,'Over it are nineteen.',NULL,74,30),
 (16586,2,'Over it are Nineteen.',NULL,74,30),
 (16587,3,'Above it are nineteen.',NULL,74,30),
 (16588,1,'And We have not made the wardens of the fire others than angels, and We have not made their number but as a trial for those who disbelieve, that those who have been given the book may be certain and those who believe may increase in faith, and those who have been given the book and the believers may not doubt, and that those in whose hearts is a disease and the unbelievers may say: What does Allah mean by this parable? Thus does Allah make err whom He pleases, and He guides whom He pleases, and none knows the hosts of your Lord but He Himself; and this is naught but a reminder to the mortals.',NULL,74,31),
 (16589,2,'And We have set none but angels as Guardians of the Fire; and We have fixed their number only as a trial for Unbelievers,- in order that the People of the Book may arrive at certainty, and the Believers may increase in Faith,- and that no doubts may be left for the People of the Book and the Believers, and that those in whose hearts is a disease and the Unbelievers may say, "What symbol doth Allah intend by this?" Thus doth Allah leave to stray whom He pleaseth, and guide whom He pleaseth: and none can know the forces of thy Lord, except He and this is no other than a warning to mankind.',NULL,74,31),
 (16590,3,'We have appointed only angels to be wardens of the Fire, and their number have We made to be a stumbling-block for those who disbelieve; that those to whom the Scripture hath been given may have certainty, and that believers may increase in faith; and that those to whom the Scripture hath been given and believers may not doubt; and that those in whose hearts there is disease, and disbelievers, may say: What meaneth Allah by this similitude? Thus Allah sendeth astray whom He will, and whom He will He guideth. None knoweth the hosts of thy Lord save Him. This is naught else than a Reminder unto mortals.',NULL,74,31),
 (16591,1,'Nay; I swear by the moon,',NULL,74,32),
